window.ATLAS_DECKS["ethics"] = {"id":"ethics","title":"An Introduction to Ethics","question":"What makes an action right or wrong?","topic":"ethics","topicName":"Ethics","minutes":45,"basis":"Draws on themes from James Rachels, The Elements of Moral Philosophy, and Simon Blackburn, Being Good","cover":"hero:2","images":{"surgery":{"src":"data:image/webp;base64,","credit":"Surgeons at work · Irene G. Ruiz · CC BY 2.0","file":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/41619404@N05/4051263576","pos":"50% 40%"},"care":{"src":"data:image/webp;base64,","credit":"Holding a baby’s hand · operabug · CC BY-NC 2.0","file":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/33458923@N08/5701874906","pos":"50% 40%"},"athens":{"src":"data:image/webp;base64,","credit":"Academy of Athens, with Plato and Socrates statues · alexdavidbaldi · CC BY-NC-SA 2.0","file":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/56813575@N05/50429094867","pos":"50% 40%"}},"slides":[{"layout":"title","kicker":"PUBLIC LECTURE · HOW SHOULD WE LIVE?","title":"An Introduction to Ethics","question":"What makes an action right or wrong?","sub":"Results, duties, character, agreement and care: five ways to answer the oldest practical question","tags":["CONSEQUENCES","DUTY","VIRTUE","CONTRACT","CARE"],"img":"hero:2","notes":"In 399 BCE Socrates was sentenced to death by the city of Athens. His friends had arranged an escape. He refused. In Plato’s dialogue the Crito he explains why: he had lived under Athens’ laws all his life, and running away would break an agreement he had kept for seventy years. Whatever you think of his choice, notice what he was doing on the last day of his life: arguing about what is right, rather than doing what was easiest.\nThat is ethics. Not a list of rules handed down, but the attempt to give reasons for how we should act and live, reasons that others can test.\nNext: what kind of question is an ethical question?"},{"layout":"columns","section":"What the field is","title":"Ethics asks what we ought to do, not what people actually do","sub":"Three levels of moral thinking","cols":[{"label":"Descriptive","title":"What do people believe?","text":"Psychology and anthropology study which moral views people actually hold, and why.","note":"Example: most people would pull the lever"},{"label":"Normative ethics","title":"What should we do?","text":"Offers and tests general theories of right action and good living: results, duties, character.","note":"Example: should you pull it?"},{"label":"Metaethics","title":"What is morality?","text":"Asks whether moral claims can be true, where they come from, and how we could know them.","note":"Example: is “you should” ever a fact?"}],"bottom":"Surveys tell us what people think is right; ethics asks whether they’re right to think it.","notes":"Three levels again. Descriptive ethics is the study of what people believe: surveys, cultures, evolution. That’s fascinating, but finding that most people believe something doesn’t show that it’s true. Normative ethics is the main event: theories of what makes acts right and lives good. And metaethics steps back further and asks what morality is. When you say torture is wrong, are you stating a fact, expressing a feeling, or reporting your culture’s rules?\nNext: the map of tonight’s questions."},{"layout":"rows","section":"A map of the field","title":"Five big theories answer one question differently","sub":"What makes an action right?","rows":[{"title":"Consequentialism","q":"The best results for everyone affected","note":"Bentham, Mill, Singer"},{"title":"Deontology","q":"Keeping duties and respecting rights","note":"Kant, Ross"},{"title":"Virtue ethics","q":"Acting as a good person would","note":"Aristotle, Confucius"},{"title":"Contractualism","q":"Following rules no one could reasonably reject","note":"Hobbes, Rawls, Scanlon"},{"title":"Care ethics","q":"Responding to the people who depend on us","note":"Gilligan, Noddings, Held"}],"bottom":"Most people mix these, but noticing which one you reach for first tells you a lot about yourself.","link":"#compass-ethics","linkLabel":"Take the ethics chapter","notes":"Here are the five main families of answer. Most of us use all of them at different times: we think about results when planning a budget, about duties when keeping a promise, about character when we ask what a good friend would do. The theories try to say which of these is most basic, and what to do when they clash.\nYou can find out which one you lean towards in the ethics chapter of the Compass.\nNext: the most famous way to make them clash."},{"layout":"image","img":"ag:killing","title":"The trolley problem","sub":"Philippa Foot, 1967; Judith Jarvis Thomson, 1976","caption":"A thought experiment","text":"A runaway tram will kill five people. You can pull a lever and send it onto a side track, where it will kill one. Most people say pull. Now imagine stopping it by pushing a large man off a footbridge into its path. Most people say no. Why, if the numbers are the same?","notes":"Philippa Foot invented this case in 1967 to think about abortion and the difference between doing and allowing harm. Judith Jarvis Thomson added the footbridge. In surveys across many countries, around 80 to 90 per cent of people say you may switch, and a large majority say you may not push, even though both save five by killing one.\nEach ethical theory has to explain that pattern, or explain it away. That makes the trolley problem a test for every theory we’ll meet.\nNext: the theory that says only results matter."},{"layout":"compare","section":"Consequences","title":"Consequentialism: the right act is the one with the best results","sub":"Everyone’s wellbeing counts, and counts equally","a":{"name":"The case for","label":"Bentham · Mill · Singer","lead":"What could matter except how lives go?","points":["Simple and impartial: no one’s suffering counts for more","Explains why lying and stealing are usually wrong: they harm","Demands real help for distant strangers"],"focus":"Pull the lever: five lives outweigh one","img":"th:mill"},"b":{"name":"The trouble","label":"Williams · Rawls · Foot","lead":"If only results matter, anything might be allowed:","points":["A surgeon could kill one healthy patient for five organs","It ignores the separateness of persons","It leaves no room for integrity or personal projects"],"focus":"Why not push the man too?","img":"surgery"},"bottom":"Consequentialism is hard to resist in theory and hard to live with in practice.","link":"#thinker-mill","linkLabel":"Meet John Stuart Mill","notes":"Jeremy Bentham and John Stuart Mill argued that the right act is the one that produces the greatest happiness, counting everyone equally. Today Peter Singer draws the demanding conclusion: if you can prevent great suffering at small cost, you must.\nThe problem is that the theory seems to allow anything if the numbers come out right. A surgeon with five dying patients and one healthy visitor could quietly harvest his organs. Bernard Williams called this a failure to take seriously the fact that each person has only one life.\nConsequentialists reply that a world where surgeons did that would be terrible, so a wise consequentialist wouldn’t. Whether that reply is enough is still debated.\nNext: the theory that says some things are simply off limits."},{"layout":"split","section":"Duty","title":"Deontology: some acts are wrong whatever the results","sub":"Immanuel Kant, Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals, 1785","challenge":{"title":"Kant’s test","text":"Act only on a rule you could will everyone to follow. Treat people never merely as means, always also as ends."},"answer":{"title":"What follows","text":"You may not lie, break promises or use one person to save others, because that treats them as a tool. This explains the footbridge: pushing the man uses him as a means. Switching the tram doesn’t aim at the one’s death."},"open":{"title":"Still unresolved","text":"Kant said you may not lie even to a murderer asking where your friend is hiding. And what if keeping a rule leads to catastrophe? Do absolute duties ever give way?"},"img":"th:kant","bottom":"Deontology protects individuals from being sacrificed, at the cost of sometimes letting disasters happen.","link":"#agora-lying","linkLabel":"Debate: is lying ever right?","notes":"Kant thought morality comes from reason itself, not from feelings or results. His test: could you will that everyone acted on your rule? A lying promise fails, because if everyone made lying promises, promises would mean nothing. His second formulation is more intuitive: never treat a person merely as a means.\nThis explains why most people won’t push the man off the bridge: you would be using his body as a tool.\nBut Kant famously said you may not lie even to a murderer at the door asking where your friend is. Many Kantians, like Christine Korsgaard, have tried to soften this. And most deontologists today, following W. D. Ross, think duties can conflict and must be weighed.\nNext: a very different starting point, not acts but character."},{"layout":"mean","section":"Character","title":"Virtue ethics: become the kind of person who acts well","sub":"Aristotle’s doctrine of the mean: each virtue lies between two vices","head":["Too little","The virtue","Too much"],"area":true,"rows":[["Cowardice","Courage","Recklessness","Facing danger"],["Meanness","Generosity","Extravagance","Giving"],["Self-deprecation","Truthfulness","Boastfulness","Talking about yourself"],["Boorishness","Wit","Buffoonery","Humour"],["Insensitivity","Temperance","Self-indulgence","Pleasure"],["Apathy","Good temper","Irascibility","Anger"]],"bottom":"The mean isn’t ‘moderation in everything’: it is the right response, at the right time, to the right person.","link":"#thinker-aristotle","linkLabel":"Meet Aristotle","notes":"Aristotle didn’t start by asking which acts are right. He asked: what kind of person should I become? His answer: someone with the virtues, settled traits of character like courage, generosity and honesty, built up by practice, the way you become a musician by playing.\nEach virtue sits between two vices. Courage lies between cowardice and recklessness. But the mean is not always the middle: sometimes the right amount of anger is a lot. It is whatever a practically wise person would feel and do in that situation.\nFor Aristotle, living virtuously is what flourishing, eudaimonia, consists in.\nNext: the same idea, independently, in China."},{"layout":"gallery","section":"Character","title":"Character-based ethics appears across the world","sub":"Different traditions, a shared focus on who we become","items":[{"img":"th:aristotle","caption":"Aristotle","text":"Virtue and flourishing (Greece, 4th c. BCE)"},{"img":"th:confucius","caption":"Confucius","text":"Ren (humaneness) and ritual (China, 5th c. BCE)"},{"img":"th:mencius","caption":"Mencius","text":"Human nature has sprouts of goodness"},{"img":"th:buddha","caption":"The Buddha","text":"Compassion and the noble eightfold path"},{"img":"th:hursthouse","caption":"Rosalind Hursthouse","text":"Modern virtue ethics: right = what the virtuous would do"},{"img":"athens","caption":"Practice makes character","text":"Virtues are learned like crafts, by doing"}],"bottom":"Ask not only ‘what should I do?’ but ‘who am I becoming by doing it?’","notes":"Character ethics isn’t only Greek. Confucius taught that a good person cultivates ren, humaneness, through ritual, learning and relationships. Mencius said every person is born with sprouts of goodness, like the instinct to rescue a child about to fall into a well, which must be nurtured like plants. Buddhist ethics trains compassion and wisdom through practice. In the twentieth century, philosophers like Elizabeth Anscombe and Rosalind Hursthouse revived virtue ethics in the West.\nThe common idea: ethics is less like following a rulebook and more like learning a skill.\nNext: what if morality is an agreement?"},{"layout":"columns","section":"Agreement and care","title":"Morality may be what we could justify to each other, or how we care for each other","sub":"Two theories that start from relationships","cols":[{"label":"Contractualism","title":"Rules no one could reasonably reject","text":"T. M. Scanlon: an act is wrong if any principle allowing it could be reasonably rejected by someone affected. Morality is about being able to justify yourself face to face.","note":"Explains rights; struggles with animals and future people","img":"th:scanlon"},{"label":"Care ethics","title":"Responding to need","text":"Carol Gilligan, Nel Noddings, Virginia Held: real moral life starts from relationships of dependence, such as parent and child or carer and patient, not from abstract strangers.","note":"Puts care work at the centre; risks favouring those close to us","img":"care"}],"bottom":"Both shift the question from ‘what maximises?’ to ‘what do we owe each other?’","notes":"Contractualism, in Scanlon’s version, says morality is about what we could justify to each other. Could the one person on the side track reasonably reject a rule that lets you divert the tram? Perhaps not. Could the man on the bridge reasonably reject a rule that lets people push him? Surely yes.\nCare ethics, developed by Carol Gilligan and Nel Noddings, criticised traditional theories for imagining morality as relations between independent, equal strangers. Most of life involves dependence: children, the sick, the old. A good theory should start there.\nNext: the case that tests how far our duties to strangers go."},{"layout":"split","section":"A hard case","title":"The drowning child: distance may not matter morally","sub":"Peter Singer, Famine, Affluence, and Morality, 1972","challenge":{"title":"The case","text":"You walk past a shallow pond where a child is drowning. Wading in will ruin your expensive shoes. Must you save the child?"},"answer":{"title":"Singer’s argument","text":"Of course you must. But the price of those shoes, given to an effective charity, can save a child far away. Distance and numbers of other bystanders don’t change your duty. So most of us should be giving much more than we do."},"open":{"title":"Objections","text":"Is there a difference between rescue and ongoing aid? Can morality demand so much that it swallows your whole life? Do we have special duties to family and neighbours first?"},"img":"ag:strangers","bottom":"If you can prevent something very bad at small cost, many think you must, wherever the victim is.","link":"#agora-strangers","linkLabel":"Debate: must we help strangers?","notes":"Peter Singer’s argument is short and hard to escape. If you can prevent something very bad without sacrificing anything of comparable moral importance, you ought to do it. Letting a child drown to save your shoes is monstrous. But a few hundred pounds given to an effective charity can save a life, and most of us spend that on things far less important.\nCritics say morality cannot be that demanding, that we have special duties to those near us, or that charity is different from rescue. Each theory you’ve met gives a different answer.\nNext: is any of this objectively true?"},{"layout":"columns","section":"Metaethics","title":"Is morality objective, or something we make?","sub":"Four answers to “is torture wrong?”","cols":[{"label":"Realism","title":"A fact, like maths","text":"Moral truths hold whatever anyone thinks (Plato, Parfit, Enoch)."},{"label":"Constructivism","title":"The result of reasoning together","text":"True, but because reasonable agents would agree, not because it’s ‘out there’ (Kant, Korsgaard, Rawls)."},{"label":"Expressivism","title":"An expression of attitude","text":"‘Wrong!’ voices disapproval and commitment, not a description (Hume, Ayer, Blackburn)."},{"label":"Error theory","title":"Always false","text":"Moral claims try to state facts, but there are none (Mackie). Not ‘anything goes’: just no moral facts."}],"bottom":"Even sceptics about moral facts usually still condemn cruelty: metaethics changes what condemning is, not whether we do it.","notes":"When you say torturing for fun is wrong, are you stating a fact? Realists say yes: there are moral truths, as there are mathematical ones. Constructivists say moral truths are what reasonable people would agree to. Expressivists, following Hume, say moral talk expresses attitudes, a kind of booing and cheering refined into reasoning. J. L. Mackie’s error theory says moral claims aim at facts that don’t exist.\nIt’s important not to misread the sceptics: Mackie didn’t think torture is fine. He thought there’s no objective property of wrongness, and that we must invent our morality and stand by it.\nNext: the whole field on one page."},{"layout":"table","section":"The whole field","title":"Every theory is strongest where the others are weakest","sub":"A map of competing reasons","head":["Theory","What it gets right","What it must answer"],"rows":[["Consequentialism","Wellbeing matters; everyone counts equally","Sacrificing individuals; demandingness"],["Deontology","Rights, dignity, promises","Catastrophes; conflicting duties"],["Virtue ethics","Character, motives, practical wisdom","Little guidance in hard cases"],["Contractualism","Justifiability to each person","Animals, children, future generations"],["Care ethics","Dependence and relationships","Fairness to strangers"]],"bottom":"Many philosophers end up pluralists, weighing results, duties and character case by case.","notes":"Put the theories side by side and something striking appears: each one is strongest exactly where the others are weakest. That’s why many philosophers, following W. D. Ross, are pluralists: several things matter, and practical wisdom weighs them. Others think one theory can be refined to absorb the truths in the rest, as Derek Parfit argued in On What Matters.\nNext: a toolkit for your own moral arguments."},{"layout":"steps","section":"Take it with you","title":"Four questions to test any moral claim","sub":"An ethical toolkit","steps":[{"title":"Universalise","text":"Would it be all right if everyone did it?"},{"title":"Count the effects","text":"Who is helped and harmed, and how much?"},{"title":"Ask about character","text":"What would doing this make of you?"},{"title":"Justify it face to face","text":"Could you explain it to the person most affected?"}],"bottom":"When all four agree, you can be confident; when they clash, you’ve found the real moral question.","notes":"Here is a practical toolkit drawn from the four theories. If everyone did it, would it work? What are the effects on everyone? What kind of person does it make you? Could you justify it to the person who loses most?\nWhen all four agree, you can be fairly confident. When they disagree, you have found the genuinely hard question, and that is where moral thinking really starts.\nNext: what ethics leaves us with."},{"layout":"closing","title":"Ethics doesn’t give us a rulebook","sub":"It leaves us with better questions","questions":["When may we sacrifice one to save many?","How much do we owe to strangers?","Is morality discovered, or made?"],"line":"The question is not just what to do, but what kind of person, and what kind of world, our choices create.","notes":"Ethics doesn’t give us a rulebook to follow without thinking. It gives us better questions and better ways to test our answers. The deepest of them may be Socrates’ own: how should one live?"},{"layout":"discussion","title":"Discussion: bring the theory back to real life","sub":"Three questions for debate","questions":["Should a self-driving car be programmed to sacrifice its passenger to save five pedestrians?","Is it wrong to eat meat if you could easily avoid it?","Should you report a friend who cheated in an exam?"],"line":"State your principle first, then test it against the other theories.","link":"#lab-gauntlet","linkLabel":"Play the Trolley Gauntlet","notes":"Three questions to argue about. For each, try answering as a consequentialist, a Kantian, a virtue ethicist and a care ethicist. Where do they agree? Where do they split? You can test your instincts on the trolley problem and its variants in the Trolley Gauntlet game."}]};
